WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.154
YOLO.

2
00:00:01.154 --> 00:00:02.500
You only look once.

3
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.618
Early detectors were accurate but glacial.

4
00:00:05.618 --> 00:00:07.967
This idea made detection real time.

5
00:00:09.167 --> 00:00:13.133
Early detectors ran a classifier over thousands of image crops.

6
00:00:13.433 --> 00:00:17.133
YOLO reframes detection as a single regression problem.

7
00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:22.033
One forward pass of one network predicts every box and every class at once.

8
00:00:23.233 --> 00:00:24.628
Here is the mechanism.

9
00:00:24.628 --> 00:00:26.467
Divide the image into a grid.

10
00:00:26.767 --> 00:00:30.776
Each cell is responsible for objects whose center falls inside it.

11
00:00:30.776 --> 00:00:32.233
This cell owns the ship.

12
00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:38.300
Every cell emits box coordinates, an objectness score, and class scores, all at the same time.

13
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.133
So the full pipeline is short.

14
00:00:41.433 --> 00:00:42.567
A frame goes in.

15
00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:45.233
The network predicts everything in one pass.

16
00:00:45.533 --> 00:00:48.533
That leaves duplicate boxes around each object.

17
00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:51.079
Non max suppression deletes them.

18
00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:57.067
One pass means real time: thirty to over a hundred and fifty frames per second on a GPU.

19
00:00:58.267 --> 00:00:59.467
Here it is in code.

20
00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:01.169
We load the nano model.

21
00:01:01.169 --> 00:01:04.033
It has only three point two million parameters.

22
00:01:04.333 --> 00:01:08.567
We run it on a harbor image and print each box's label and confidence.

23
00:01:08.867 --> 00:01:13.200
And training on your own labeled data is a single call with a config file.

24
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.333
Here's where speed is the requirement.

25
00:01:16.633 --> 00:01:20.319
A satellite service must post ship counts before the nine a.

26
00:01:20.319 --> 00:01:20.442
m.

27
00:01:20.442 --> 00:01:20.933
standup.

28
00:01:21.233 --> 00:01:28.133
A YOLO model fine-tuned on fifteen hundred annotated tiles handles the forty thousand tile scene in minutes.

29
00:01:28.433 --> 00:01:33.667
A two stage detector would triple the runtime for accuracy you don't need to count ships.

30
00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:35.458
To recap.

31
00:01:35.458 --> 00:01:39.267
YOLO treats detection as one dense prediction over a grid.

32
00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:43.133
Each cell outputs a box, an objectness score and classes.

33
00:01:43.433 --> 00:01:45.100
One pass means real time.

34
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.986
It's the practical default for live video and edge.

35
00:01:48.986 --> 00:01:50.533
Next up: Faster R-CNN.
